,"title":"IncidenceMatrixModule","identifier":"doc:\/\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/documentation\/IncidenceMatrixModule","kind":"symbol","type":"topic","role":"collection"},"doc://IncidenceMatrixModule/documentation/IncidenceMatrixModule/OrientedIncidenceMatrix":{"type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/documentation\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/OrientedIncidenceMatrix","title":"OrientedIncidenceMatrix","url":"\/documentation\/incidencematrixmodule\/orientedincidencematrix","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The oriented incidence matrix of a directed hypergraph: entry (v, p) is −1 when "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"v"},{"type":"text","text":" is a source"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"of column "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"p"},{"type":"text","text":", +1 when it is a target, and 0 otherwise. Gallo, Longo, Pallottino and Nguyen"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"(1993) define a directed hypergraph’s matrix this way (aᵢⱼ = −1 for a tail node, 1 for a head"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"node); a directed graph’s edge is the column with one source and one target, the matrix of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"spectral graph theory (B·Bᵀ = L, Godsil–Royle §8.3) and NetworkX’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"incidence_matrix(oriented=True)"},{"type":"text","text":". It conforms to "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DirectedHypergraph"},{"type":"text","text":", so the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Hypergraphs"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"algorithms (B- and F-connection, components, the clique expansion) run on it unchanged; it is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"not a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DirectedGraph"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"OrientedIncidenceMatrix","kind":"identifier"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"OrientedIncidenceMatrix","kind":"identifier"}],"kind":"symbol","role":"symbol"},"doc://IncidenceMatrixModule/documentation/IncidenceMatrixModule/OrientedIncidenceMatrix/Equatable-Implementations":{"url":"\/documentation\/incidencematrixmodule\/orientedincidencematrix\/equatable-implementations","title":"Equatable Implementations","abstract":[],"type":"topic","kind":"article","identifier":"doc:\/\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/documentation\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/OrientedIncidenceMatrix\/Equatable-Implementations","role":"collectionGroup"},"doc://GraphProtocols/documentation/GraphProtocols/DirectedHypergraph":{"title":"DirectedHypergraph","kind":"symbol","type":"topic","role":"symbol","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A directed hypergraph: a finite set of vertices and a finite collection of hyperedges, each an"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"ordered pair of vertex sets, its sources (tail) and its targets (head). Gallo, Longo, Pallottino"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"and Nguyen, “Directed hypergraphs and applications”, "},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Discrete Applied Mathematics","type":"text"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"42(2–3):177–201, 1993, define a hyperarc as “an ordered pair … of (possibly empty) disjoint"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"subsets”; here either side may be empty and a vertex may be on both, the hypergraph form of a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"self-loop (XGI’s "},{"code":"DiHypergraph","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" allows both)."}],"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"protocol"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"DirectedHypergraph"},{"kind":"text","text":"<"},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Vertex","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols18DirectedHypergraphP6VertexQa"},{"kind":"text","text":">"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedHypergraph","url":"\/documentation\/graphprotocols\/directedhypergraph"},"doc://IncidenceMatrixModule/documentation/IncidenceMatrixModule/OrientedIncidenceMatrix/subscript(vertex:hyperedge:)":{"fragments":[{"text":"subscript","kind":"keyword"},{"text":"(","kind":"text"},{"text":"vertex","kind":"externalParam"},{"text":" _: ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Int","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:Si"},{"text":", ","kind":"text"},{"text":"hyperedge","kind":"externalParam"},{"text":" _: ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Int","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:Si"},{"text":") -> ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Int","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:Si"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Entry ("},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertex"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"position"},{"type":"text","text":"): target − source, so −1 for a source, +1 for a target, and 0"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"for neither or both. O(1)."}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/documentation\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/OrientedIncidenceMatrix\/subscript(vertex:hyperedge:)","kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/incidencematrixmodule\/orientedincidencematrix\/subscript(vertex:hyperedge:)","type":"topic","title":"subscript(vertex:hyperedge:)","role":"symbol"},"doc://IncidenceMatrixModule/s8CopyableP":{"title":"Swift.Copyable","type":"unresolvable","identifier":"doc:\/\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/s8CopyableP"},"doc://IncidenceMatrixModule/documentation/IncidenceMatrixModule/OrientedIncidenceMatrix/DirectedHypergraph-Implementations":{"title":"DirectedHypergraph Implementations","kind":"article","type":"topic","role":"collectionGroup","abstract":[],"identifier":"doc:\/\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/documentation\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/OrientedIncidenceMatrix\/DirectedHypergraph-Implementations","url":"\/documentation\/incidencematrixmodule\/orientedincidencematrix\/directedhypergraph-implementations"},"doc://IncidenceMatrixModule/documentation/IncidenceMatrixModule/OrientedInciden